{"id": "tea:madhyamakahrdaya:3.12", "source": "src:madhyamakahrdaya", "location": {"ref": "3.12", "chapter": "3", "verse": "12"}, "paraphrase": "Without the ladder of the correct conventional truth, the wise cannot climb to the top of the mansion of reality.", "tags": {"level": "bridging", "standpoint": "seeker", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["ultimate", "practice"], "concepts": ["cpt:two-truths", "cpt:conventional-truth-divisions"], "notes": "Verse number from memory.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:bhaviveka"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakahrdaya:5", "source": "src:madhyamakahrdaya", "location": {"ref": "5"}, "paraphrase": "Chapter on the Yogācāra: the Yogācāras' claim that only consciousness exists and that the other-dependent nature is real is examined and rejected; consciousness too lacks own-nature ultimately, while external things are admitted conventionally.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["dispute"], "disputes": ["dsp:madhyamaka-yogacara"], "concepts": ["cpt:madhyamaka-critique-of-yogacara"], "notes": "Chapter-level summary from memory.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:bhaviveka"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakahrdaya:6", "source": "src:madhyamakahrdaya", "location": {"ref": "6"}, "paraphrase": "Chapter on the Sāṃkhya: the Sāṃkhyas' primal matter and the pre-existence of the effect in the cause are examined; an effect already existing needs no production, and an eternal, unconscious pradhāna cannot be the cause of the world.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["dispute"], "disputes": ["dsp:causation"], "concepts": ["cpt:madhyamaka-critique-of-samkhya"], "terms": ["trm:satkaryavada"], "notes": "Chapter-level summary from memory.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:bhaviveka"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakahrdaya:8", "source": "src:madhyamakahrdaya", "location": {"ref": "8"}, "paraphrase": "(The Vedānta view as Bhāviveka reports it:) the supreme person (puruṣa), the self, is one, all-pervading, eternal and the lord; beings arise from it and dissolve into it; it is known by yogins who see it in themselves, and knowing it one attains immortality.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["dispute", "ultimate"], "reported_by_opponent": true, "notes": "Pūrvapakṣa of the Vedāntatattvaviniścaya chapter, known here through Bhāviveka's (opponent's) report; wording from memory.", "disputes": ["dsp:is-there-a-self"], "concepts": ["cpt:madhyamaka-critique-of-vedanta"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:bhaviveka"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakahrdaya:8/2", "source": "src:madhyamakahrdaya", "location": {"ref": "8/2"}, "paraphrase": "(Reply:) such a permanent and single self could neither act, change nor be known, and cannot be the cause of the many; whatever in the Vedāntins' teaching is acceptable — non-duality, freedom from conceptual construction — is acceptable only as the emptiness taught by the Buddha.", "tags": {"level": "ultimate", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["dispute"], "disputes": ["dsp:is-there-a-self"], "concepts": ["cpt:madhyamaka-critique-of-vedanta"], "notes": "Chapter-level summary from memory; the claim that the Vedāntins' good points derive from Buddhism is attributed to this chapter in modern summaries — to check.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:bhaviveka"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:madhyamakahrdaya:9", "source": "src:madhyamakahrdaya", "location": {"ref": "9"}, "paraphrase": "Chapter on the Mīmāṃsā: the Mīmāṃsakas' view that the Veda is authorless and eternal and that no one can be omniscient is examined; words and scriptures depend on speakers, and a teacher who has seen reality directly is possible.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["dispute", "sound-language"], "disputes": ["dsp:status-of-veda"], "concepts": ["cpt:madhyamaka-critique-of-mimamsa"], "notes": "Chapter-level summary from memory.", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U40-madhyamaka"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "teachers": ["tch:bhaviveka"], "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U40-madhyamaka"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
